        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Verify that the set of <m>2 \times 2</m> real matrices form a vector space over 
                   <m>\R</m>. Identify a basis of this vector space, and use it to determine the dimension of the vector space.
                </p>
                
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Are the set of <m>2 \times 2</m> real matrices a vector space over <m>\C</m>? If so, determine a basis 
                    and the dimension. If not, explain why.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                     Are the set of <m>m \times n</m> complex matrices a vector space over <m>\C</m>? If so, determine a basis 
                    and the dimension. If not, explain why.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
        Let 
        <md>
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
            2 \amp 2 \amp 2
            \end{bmatrix}
        </md>.


        
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Find all solutions to <m>A\xvec = \mathbf{0}</m>. This is call the <term>null space</term> of <m>A</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The null space of a matrix is itself a vector space (it is closed under addition and scalar multiplication).
                    Use your solution above to identify a basis for the null space of <m>A</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        
    </exercise>
